  "interpretation": "The kernel frees a FIFO node by storing state 0xff before clearing data_size. The HAL reuses a node after an ordinary load sees 0xff and can then reset/increment that size. An interleaving can therefore let the kernel's later zero erase a producer update. In the opposite direction, the HAL writes payload and size then state U without an explicit release, while the kernel uses ordinary state and size loads without an acquire. These observations identify an unproven two-way shared-memory handoff and a static concurrency window, not a reproduced failure.",
